•	These programs uniquely focus on emerging technologies and responsible innovation, going beyond just artificial intelligence to include areas like digital twins and quantum computing.
•	This offering is currently the only one of its kind in New South Wales, distinguishing it from other computer science programs in the region.
How does this program compare to others?
•	The entry requirements are more inclusive, with a lower ATAR requirement of 75 compared to similar programs like the University of Queensland's, which requires an ATAR of around 95.
•	The curriculum stands out by emphasising responsible innovation and a broader range of emerging technologies, not limiting itself to just AI.
•	A key differentiator is the deep integration of industry partnerships throughout the curriculum, providing students with real-world exposure and opportunities.
What are the key focus areas?
•	The program covers a range of emerging technologies including artificial intelligence, digital twins, and quantum computing, preparing students for future technological developments.
•	There's a strong emphasis on responsible innovation, particularly in areas like cybersecurity and digital trust, ensuring students consider ethical implications of technology.
•	The curriculum adopts a human-centred approach, focusing on using technology to address real-world societal issues.
Industry Partnerships
Which industry partners are involved?
•	Major technology companies such as AWS, Cisco, Microsoft, and Palo Alto are key partners in the program.
•	These partners are deeply involved, providing input on curriculum design and supporting the delivery of course content.
•	The university is exploring opportunities for these partners to offer sponsorships and scholarships to students, enhancing the value proposition of the program.
What certifications can students earn?
•	Content from industry partners, particularly AWS, is integrated into the curriculum, aligning with industry certification requirements.
•	After completing relevant units, students will be well-prepared to sit for industry certifications, such as those offered by AWS.
•	This integration facilitates easier achievement of valuable industry certifications alongside the degree.
What career opportunities are available?
•	The program aims to provide internship opportunities with industry partners, giving students practical experience.
•	There's potential for graduate roles with these partner organisations upon completion of the degree.
•	Students in this program may have an advantage in securing positions at partner organisations due to their familiarity with the companies' technologies and practices.
Curriculum and Teaching Approach
How is responsible innovation incorporated?
•	Ethical considerations are woven throughout the curriculum, ensuring students consistently engage with responsible technology development.
•	Students will learn and apply a framework for ensuring technology is developed and used responsibly.
•	Key concepts like accountability, privacy, and accessibility are emphasised in the context of AI and other technology development.
What is the teaching approach?
•	The program uses an active learning approach, incorporating pre-class, in-class, and post-class activities to engage students fully.
•	The curriculum is informed by both current research and industry practices, ensuring relevance and currency.
•	There's multidisciplinary collaboration with other faculties at the university, providing a well-rounded perspective on technology applications.
What support is provided to students?
•	The program offers comprehensive support including informational, emotional, and tangible assistance to students.
•	There's a focus on enhancing students' psychological well-being, recognising its impact on academic performance.
•	For online students, support includes webinars, one-on-one consultations, and AI-powered tools providing 24/7 assistance.
Program Differentiators
What makes this program unique?
•	Industry partners are embedded throughout the curriculum, going beyond occasional guest lectures to provide ongoing input and support.
•	The program's focus extends beyond AI to a broader range of emerging technologies, preparing students for future developments.
•	There's a strong emphasis on responsible, ethical, and human-centred approaches to technology development.
•	The curriculum balances technical skills with soft skills development, recognising the importance of both in the industry.
What research informs the curriculum?
•	The program is informed by research into security hubs, exploring how to support those affected by cybersecurity threats.
•	Work on developing a national cybersecurity framework influences the curriculum content.
•	Research into digital identity for medical records contributes to the program's focus on practical applications of technology.
Target Audience
Who is the program aimed at?
•	While historically computer science programs have attracted more male students, this program aims to encourage greater female participation in tech fields.
•	The program caters to both recent school leavers and working professionals seeking to upskill or change careers.
•	It's designed to accommodate those unable to attend face-to-face classes, offering flexibility for various life circumstances.
•	The program also appeals to organisations looking to upskill their employees in emerging technologies and data science.
What motivates students to choose this program?
•	The double degree offers the opportunity to earn a master's degree in 4 years, which is typically faster than traditional pathways.
•	The lower ATAR requirement makes the program more accessible to a wider range of students.
•	Students receive a high level of support throughout the program, enhancing their learning experience.
•	The program highlights potential career opportunities with competitive salaries in the tech industry.
•	Unlike some programs, there's no advanced mathematics prerequisite, only assumed knowledge is needed, making it more accessible to students from diverse educational backgrounds.


